WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.210
<v Instructor>So let's get started with IAM.</v>

2
00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:04.260
So first there's a lot of things

3
00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:05.750
you should know about IAM already.

4
00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.610
So I'm not gonna go over the basics.

5
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:09.470
I will just try to remind you what it is.

6
00:00:09.470 --> 00:00:11.910
So users have long term credentials

7
00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.140
and therefore you're AWS users.

8
00:00:14.140 --> 00:00:15.380
You can group them

9
00:00:15.380 --> 00:00:16.590
and you can also define roles

10
00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:18.320
which are going to be short term credentials

11
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.530
and then we use the STS service to endorse these roles

12
00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.710
and get credentials that are gonna be temporary

13
00:00:23.710 --> 00:00:25.240
out of those, to do the actions

14
00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.490
that the roles are authorized to do.

15
00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.220
A specific kinda role we've been seeing

16
00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.480
all along in the courses is the EC2 instance role

17
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.420
where it's going to use the EC2 metadata service

18
00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.320
to get these short-term credentials on an EC2 instance.

19
00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:41.030
And you can assign only one role at a time for the instance.

20
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:43.310
But this way, your instance can access for example,

21
00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:46.890
an S3 Bucket or DynamoDB table and so on.

22
00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.130
There's also service roles

23
00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:50.200
which are assigned to services directly.

24
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:54.003
So if you have a service such as API Gateway or CodeDeploy

25
00:00:54.003 --> 00:00:57.020
that needs to do an action on an Auto Scaling Group

26
00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:59.320
or a Lambda Function or something else,

27
00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:01.770
then that service needs to have a role

28
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.010
and that role needs to be able

29
00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:05.920
and provisioned to do all the actions it needs.

30
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.970
Okay and then finally we have Cross Account roles.

31
00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:11.120
And these roles are going to be really helpful

32
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:13.870
in case you need to access another account

33
00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.460
to perform actions in that other account.

34
00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:19.860
You never share user's credentials Cross Account,

35
00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.050
you always allow to assume roles

36
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:24.790
and we'll see this in details in this course.

37
00:01:24.790 --> 00:01:26.050
Now you have policies in IAM

38
00:01:26.050 --> 00:01:29.650
and they will define what a role or a user can do.

39
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.880
And so you have three kinds,

40
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.560
You have AWS Managed,

41
00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:35.060
which is policies defined by AWS

42
00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.390
that are going to be changing over time, maybe,

43
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:39.800
but they will do something very specific.

44
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:43.350
Customer Managed, which is you creating these policies

45
00:01:43.350 --> 00:01:46.270
and you can assign them to multiple users or multiple roles

46
00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.950
and you can make them evolve over time, version them.

47
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:51.400
Or Inline Policies, which are going to be policies

48
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.000
assigned to one specific user or one specific role

49
00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.410
and you can make them evolve over time

50
00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:59.350
but you cannot share them across users or across roles.

51
00:01:59.350 --> 00:02:00.900
And finally we'll do a discussion

52
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.600
on resource based policies.

53
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:04.920
So this is when you have an S3 Bucket policy

54
00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:07.410
or an SQS queue policy and so on,

55
00:02:07.410 --> 00:02:09.250
which would allow us to perform

56
00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.390
some really interesting patterns

57
00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.620
and we'll see this in this lecture.

58
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.550
So what does an IAM policy look like?

59
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:18.580
Well, first of all it's going to be JSON document

60
00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:20.840
and they will have four things or five things,

61
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.150
Effects, Action, Resources, Conditions

62
00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.690
and sometimes in it, Policy Variables.

63
00:02:26.690 --> 00:02:28.350
We'll do a deep dive in all of those.

64
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.620
But the idea is that a JSON policy looks like this.

65
00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:33.060
And then we have some statement,

66
00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.540
for example allow EC2 attach volume,

67
00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.260
EC2 detach volume on the resource

68
00:02:37.260 --> 00:02:39.270
which is all EC2 instances.

69
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.110
And the condition is string equals

70
00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:42.880
resource type department development.

71
00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:45.570
That means that only the EC2 instances

72
00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:47.980
tagged with this tag can be attached

73
00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:50.120
or detached to volume and so on.

74
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.230
So this is quite specific

75
00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:54.770
and you can get very, very crazy with IAM policies

76
00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:56.790
but this is how all of AWS works

77
00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:58.070
and we know this already.

78
00:02:58.070 --> 00:03:01.840
If there is an explicit DENY in your IAM policy

79
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:04.547
then it will have precedence over any ALLOW

80
00:03:04.547 --> 00:03:06.670
and so that means that explicit DENY's

81
00:03:06.670 --> 00:03:08.600
always have the highest priority,

82
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.840
and we'll have a lecture exactly

83
00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.810
to understand how IAM policies

84
00:03:11.810 --> 00:03:15.100
and everything else are evaluated in order.

85
00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:17.210
Okay so the best practice we know this already

86
00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:20.500
is to use the least privilege for maximum security.

87
00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:21.990
That means that you need to make sure

88
00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.860
that the IAM policies are allowed

89
00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:27.220
just to do what they need to be doing and not more.

90
00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.180
Some tools we can use to make sure that this is the case,

91
00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.470
there is IAM Access Advisor

92
00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.120
where you can see all the permissions

93
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.930
you have granted to an IAM policy.

94
00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:37.940
And the last time each of these permissions

95
00:03:37.940 --> 00:03:39.390
was last accessed.

96
00:03:39.390 --> 00:03:41.990
So in case you have a policy

97
00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.800
or something that was not used for a year

98
00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.850
maybe it's worth removing it from the IAM policy

99
00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:49.610
to ensure there is less privilege.

100
00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.600
Okay, another one is going to be Access Analyzer

101
00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:54.590
and this is to analyze resources are shared

102
00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:56.420
with external entities, for example,

103
00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.310
S3 Buckets and this will allow you

104
00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:01.890
to look at if other accounts have access to your S3 Bucket,

105
00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:03.640
maybe there's something you're not expecting here

106
00:04:03.640 --> 00:04:07.370
and you want to make sure to lock down that S3 bucket.

107
00:04:07.370 --> 00:04:09.880
Okay, if you're not very familiar with IAM policy

108
00:04:09.880 --> 00:04:12.620
I would encourage you to go to this URL

109
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.030
to make sure you look at a few of them

110
00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.410
and understand them better.

111
00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:16.700
But I would assume that by now

112
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:19.450
you know what they look like already and how they work.

113
00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:23.520
A few common IAM policies we'll get across

114
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:25.650
is going to be the AdministratorAccess

115
00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.770
which is meaning that everything

116
00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.430
should be allowed on any resource.

117
00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.450
And so this is a policy you have to specify

118
00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.990
so by default, if you set nothing in an IAM policy

119
00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:37.980
then everything will be denied.

120
00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.450
But if you add that statement,

121
00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.910
which is, allow action star resource star,

122
00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.240
then everything will be allowed

123
00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.450
and that will give you AdministratorAccess.

124
00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.440
And because this is something very common

125
00:04:47.440 --> 00:04:51.970
for administrators, AWS provides this as a Managed Policy.

126
00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:53.660
Another very interesting policy,

127
00:04:53.660 --> 00:04:56.310
a bit less privileged, is called AWS PowerUserAccess.

128
00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.243
And so in the first one,

129
00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:02.720
the "Effect" is "Allow", "Not action"

130
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.490
on IAM star organizations

131
00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:06.060
and account star for resource stars,

132
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.790
so that means that it's not going to allow

133
00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:13.270
anything to be done on IAM organizations and accounts.

134
00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:15.490
And also it's going to allow still

135
00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:18.990
a few IAM actions such as create service link role,

136
00:05:18.990 --> 00:05:20.560
delete service link role, list roles,

137
00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:23.050
describe organization and list regions.

138
00:05:23.050 --> 00:05:24.400
So you may be asking me,

139
00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.970
why on the left hand side

140
00:05:25.970 --> 00:05:29.960
is "NotAction" used and not just "Effect":"Deny."

141
00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:32.160
So if you use "Effect":"Deny,"

142
00:05:32.160 --> 00:05:34.950
and then you specify these three actions

143
00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:37.160
and then you specify "Effect":"Allow,"

144
00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:38.830
and these five actions.

145
00:05:38.830 --> 00:05:41.140
These five actions will automatically be denied

146
00:05:41.140 --> 00:05:42.870
because there will be an explicit deny

147
00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:44.230
on the left of hand side.

148
00:05:44.230 --> 00:05:47.140
So here we have a very interesting use case,

149
00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:50.520
in case we don't want to deny everything in there

150
00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.840
because we want to explicitly allow a few things in there,

151
00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:57.080
then we can use the "NotAction" instead of "Deny"

152
00:05:57.080 --> 00:06:00.700
to allow for these two things to coexist together.

153
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.650
Okay next, conditions in IAM policies.

154
00:06:04.650 --> 00:06:06.660
So this is what a condition would look like.

155
00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:08.780
So we have a condition operator, a condition key

156
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:10.050
and a condition value.

157
00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:13.590
And so we can do a lot of crazy things with conditions.

158
00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.900
The first one is going to be a string operator,

159
00:06:15.900 --> 00:06:18.870
so for example, you're saying I want my principle tag

160
00:06:18.870 --> 00:06:21.350
to be job category: IAM user admin,

161
00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:23.210
so this is when you look at tags.

162
00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:24.610
Or you can look at, for example,

163
00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.080
on this pre-prefix for this pre-Bucket policy

164
00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:28.460
to say that we want the users

165
00:06:28.460 --> 00:06:31.950
to only access a specific home directory.

166
00:06:31.950 --> 00:06:33.670
There's numeric operators

167
00:06:33.670 --> 00:06:35.900
where we can look at, is it equal,

168
00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.170
Equal than or not equal and so on.

169
00:06:38.170 --> 00:06:40.690
Dates to look at, compare dates, this is very helpful

170
00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.800
when you want to provide temporary access

171
00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:44.800
to a specific service.

172
00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:46.940
Booleans which is going to be really helpful

173
00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.400
for example, if you want to evaluate SSL

174
00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.640
such as Secure Transport: true,

175
00:06:51.640 --> 00:06:54.040
and also when you want to look at MFA

176
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:57.460
using Multifactor Authentication present: true.

177
00:06:57.460 --> 00:06:59.300
There's also an IP address condition

178
00:06:59.300 --> 00:07:01.120
or not IP address condition.

179
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:04.060
And these conditions will be really helpful for buckets,

180
00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:05.660
S3 bucket policies for example

181
00:07:05.660 --> 00:07:06.790
or any kind of policy

182
00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:09.340
we want to ensure that only a specific kind

183
00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:12.080
of source IP can access the service.

184
00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:15.140
So we can have an IP address source IP equals,

185
00:07:15.140 --> 00:07:18.570
and here we have a CIDR block slash 24.

186
00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:21.500
And finally, we can look at ArnEquals, ArnLike

187
00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.090
and the Null condition.

188
00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.903
We're also checking if something is null or not.

189
00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.150
Now, right now, I just wanna reassure you

190
00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:31.470
you don't have to remember all these things okay?

191
00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:33.920
I just want to show you these things exist

192
00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:37.230
so that you know you can have very specific IAM policies

193
00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.640
such as if the exam is asking you to use an IAM policy

194
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:42.020
as an answer to a problem,

195
00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:43.700
this would be possible instead of using,

196
00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:45.600
for example a custom script okay?

197
00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.170
But you don't remember exactly all these things

198
00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.710
the exact conditions and so on.

199
00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:53.860
Okay next, there is Policy Variables and Tags

200
00:07:53.860 --> 00:07:55.600
and these are really, really powerful.

201
00:07:55.600 --> 00:07:59.030
One we've seen is dollar sign AWS username

202
00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:00.640
and this is most commonly used,

203
00:08:00.640 --> 00:08:03.410
for example in S3 buckets policy.

204
00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:06.570
So we have resource minus S3 buckets, slash

205
00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:10.093
the variable AWS username, slash star.

206
00:08:10.093 --> 00:08:12.790
And what this does is that it will allow your username

207
00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.520
to perform actions, all the actions on just the prefix

208
00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:20.210
that starts with AWS username.

209
00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.660
And so that means that every user

210
00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:25.489
will have its own little directory in your S3 bucket.

211
00:08:25.489 --> 00:08:27.800
And that is a very common usage for this.

212
00:08:27.800 --> 00:08:31.150
There is some AWS specific Policy Variables and Tags,

213
00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:34.060
for example CurrentTime, TokenIssueTime,

214
00:08:34.060 --> 00:08:36.910
principal type, secure transport, which is for SSL,

215
00:08:36.910 --> 00:08:40.290
source IP, user ID and source instance ARN.

216
00:08:40.290 --> 00:08:42.940
And these are provided by AWS.

217
00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:45.350
There's service specific policy roles and tags,

218
00:08:45.350 --> 00:08:50.350
for example S3 prefix, max keys, SNS endpoint, SNS protocol.

219
00:08:50.810 --> 00:08:54.040
And finally, you can have tag based policy variables.

220
00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.950
For example, if you use resource tag, slash key name

221
00:08:56.950 --> 00:09:00.080
or principle tag, slash key name and so on

222
00:09:00.080 --> 00:09:03.920
so you can start having some really powerful IAM policies.

223
00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:05.550
And this is why I would encourage you to use the link

224
00:09:05.550 --> 00:09:07.390
from before to just look at a few of them

225
00:09:07.390 --> 00:09:08.700
and try to understand them.

226
00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.570
So you can really see the whole range

227
00:09:10.570 --> 00:09:12.243
of what can be done in IAM.

228
00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.660
Okay, more importantly, and more exam focused

229
00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:17.950
is going to be the difference of IAM roles

230
00:09:17.950 --> 00:09:19.750
and resource based policies.

231
00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.320
And it may not be very clear exactly what the difference is

232
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.830
but there is a very, very clear difference.

233
00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:26.710
So we have two options right?

234
00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.560
We can attach a policy to resource,

235
00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:29.760
for example, in S3 bucket

236
00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.990
versus attaching of using a role as a proxy.

237
00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:35.520
So here is an example.

238
00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:37.600
We have a user in account A

239
00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.080
and is trying to access an S3 bucket in account B.

240
00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.160
Now there's two ways of doing this, right?

241
00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:46.310
The first one is to use a role in account B

242
00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:49.720
and we'll be assuming that role from account A

243
00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.560
and once you assume the role in account B

244
00:09:51.560 --> 00:09:53.860
the role will have the necessary permissions

245
00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:55.730
to access the S3 buckets.

246
00:09:55.730 --> 00:09:57.070
And that works.

247
00:09:57.070 --> 00:10:00.860
Another option is to use an S3 bucket policy

248
00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:02.700
and in the S3 bucket policy, we're saying,

249
00:10:02.700 --> 00:10:06.330
okay user in account A can access

250
00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:09.070
my Amazon S3 bucket in account B.

251
00:10:09.070 --> 00:10:12.370
And both these solutions allow user account A

252
00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:15.050
to access the Amazon S3 bucket in account B.

253
00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:19.070
But there is a very, very big difference.

254
00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:20.130
What is it?

255
00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:22.246
It is that when you assume a role,

256
00:10:22.246 --> 00:10:24.440
whether it be a user, an application or a service

257
00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.680
you're going to give up all your original permissions

258
00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.700
and you're going to take the permissions

259
00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:31.660
assigned to the role.

260
00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:35.130
So that means that before, when we do assume the role

261
00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:37.900
and accounts B, the orange situation

262
00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:41.030
when the user in account A, gives up all his permissions

263
00:10:41.030 --> 00:10:44.663
in account A while it's being assuming a role in account B.

264
00:10:45.680 --> 00:10:47.730
So the idea is that when you assume a role

265
00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.420
as a user application or service,

266
00:10:49.420 --> 00:10:50.290
then you're going to give up

267
00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:52.390
your original permissions and you're going to take

268
00:10:52.390 --> 00:10:54.380
the permissions assigned to the role.

269
00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:56.440
And when you're using, on the contrary,

270
00:10:56.440 --> 00:10:59.050
a resource based policy then the principal

271
00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:01.500
does not have to give up any permissions.

272
00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.710
So when do we use resource based policy?

273
00:11:03.710 --> 00:11:05.680
Well, here is in this complex example, an example.

274
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:08.870
So we have user in account A that needs to scan

275
00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.600
a DynamoDB in account A so it's own account.

276
00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:13.380
But then dump the content of it

277
00:11:13.380 --> 00:11:15.410
in an S3 bucket in account B.

278
00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:18.520
As you can see the user needs to operate on two accounts.

279
00:11:18.520 --> 00:11:21.110
Well, in this case, we need an IAM role in account A

280
00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:23.730
to make it happen, and also a resource policy

281
00:11:23.730 --> 00:11:25.890
on the S3 bucket and account B to make it happen.

282
00:11:25.890 --> 00:11:27.410
So this is a very, very good use case

283
00:11:27.410 --> 00:11:28.940
for resource based policies.

284
00:11:28.940 --> 00:11:31.080
So resource based policies are supported

285
00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:33.870
by so many AWS services and is increasing,

286
00:11:33.870 --> 00:11:36.790
so for example, Amazon industry buckets, SNS topics

287
00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:39.690
SQS queues, Lambda functions, ECR, Backup,

288
00:11:39.690 --> 00:11:41.150
EFS, Glacier, Cloud9.

289
00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.090
I mean, you'll see the list here,

290
00:11:43.090 --> 00:11:44.520
is that it's pretty much supported

291
00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:48.033
by most of the important resources in AWS.

292
00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:50.930
Now let's talk about, IAM permission boundaries.

293
00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:53.070
So we know that there are some permission boundaries

294
00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:56.350
and they're supported for users and roles, but not groups.

295
00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.660
This is an advanced features that is going to

296
00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.450
allow you to set the maximum permissions

297
00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:02.890
an IAM entity can get.

298
00:12:02.890 --> 00:12:04.590
So an example is going to be way easier.

299
00:12:04.590 --> 00:12:08.360
So for example, say we attach this IAM permission boundary

300
00:12:08.360 --> 00:12:11.860
so saying S3 star, clover star and EC2 star

301
00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:13.230
and this IAM permission.

302
00:12:13.230 --> 00:12:14.860
So through this IAM policy

303
00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:18.170
we just allow IAM create user star, okay.

304
00:12:18.170 --> 00:12:21.080
Well, these two things together will mix up.

305
00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:22.030
And then what will happen

306
00:12:22.030 --> 00:12:23.550
is that there will be no permissions.

307
00:12:23.550 --> 00:12:25.250
Well, because even though the action

308
00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:28.120
IAM create user is authorized on the right hand side,

309
00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:29.470
because the IAM permission boundaries

310
00:12:29.470 --> 00:12:32.300
only allow the permissions on S3 CloudWatch, and ec2

311
00:12:32.300 --> 00:12:35.260
then the user zero will have no permissions.

312
00:12:35.260 --> 00:12:37.910
So IAM permission boundaries can also be used

313
00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:40.240
with AWS organization SCP.

314
00:12:40.240 --> 00:12:42.380
So if you look at the SCP in the middle

315
00:12:42.380 --> 00:12:45.430
we have the intersection of the SCP,

316
00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:48.640
the permission boundary and the identity based policies.

317
00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:50.280
So you would use IAM permission boundaries

318
00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:51.900
when you want to delegate responsibility

319
00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:54.970
to non-administrator within their permission boundaries,

320
00:12:54.970 --> 00:12:57.090
for example to create new IAM users

321
00:12:57.090 --> 00:12:59.050
or to allow developers to self assign policy

322
00:12:59.050 --> 00:13:01.000
to themselves and manage their own permissions

323
00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.340
while making sure that they can only do these things

324
00:13:03.340 --> 00:13:05.300
within their privilege rights.

325
00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.430
So they cannot escalate themselves.

326
00:13:07.430 --> 00:13:09.400
And it's also very useful to restrict one

327
00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:12.750
specific user instead of using a whole account

328
00:13:12.750 --> 00:13:15.300
restriction with organizations and SCP.

329
00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:16.680
So that's it for IAM, hopefully you know

330
00:13:16.680 --> 00:13:17.730
most of these things

331
00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.140
and I will see you in the next lecture.

